<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1953</h1><p class="ref-lede">1953 in world history: 16 events, 15 births, 13 deaths, 3 works, 4 states founded and 3 that came to an end. 361 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a><a href="/years/1950">1950</a><a href="/years/1951">1951</a><a href="/years/1952">1952</a><a href="/years/1953" aria-current="page">1953</a><a href="/years/1954">1954</a><a href="/years/1955">1955</a><a href="/years/1956">1956</a><a href="/years/1957">1957</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-7">7 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Truman announces the United States has built a hydrogen bomb</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In his final State of the Union message, Harry Truman disclosed that the US had developed the hydrogen bomb, tested in November 1952. Its power dwarfed the atomic bombs used against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-28">28 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Watson and Crick work out the structure of DNA</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At the Cavendish Laboratory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Francis Crick and James Watson arrived at the double-helix model of DNA, drawing on Rosalind Franklin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> data. They published it in April.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Joseph Stalin dies</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Soviet dictator died four days after a stroke at his dacha near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. His rule had brought rapid industrialisation and victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but also famine, purges and millions of deaths.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-8">8 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jomo Kenyatta is convicted at the Kapenguria trial</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A colonial court in Kenya sentenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> and five others to seven years' hard labour for allegedly managing Mau Mau. The widely criticised verdict made him a national symbol; he became Kenya's first president.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Watson and Crick publish the double helix</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Nature published James Watson and Francis Crick's paper on the structure of DNA, alongside papers by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin's group. It explained how genetic information is copied.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-29">29 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Hillary and Tenzing reach the summit of Everest</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Edmund Hillary</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenzing-norgay">Tenzing Norgay</a>, a Sherpa from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, became the first climbers confirmed to have reached the summit of the world's highest mountain.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Elizabeth II is crowned at Westminster Abbey</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The coronation was the first to be televised in full; some 27 million people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> watched, many on newly bought sets. It was a landmark in the spread of television.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Julius and Ethel Rosenberg are executed</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The couple were electrocuted at Sing Sing for passing atomic secrets to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Ethel's role, in particular, remains disputed.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Fidel Castro leads the attack on the Moncada Barracks</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> led some 160 rebels against the Moncada Barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The attack failed, but his trial speech 'History Will Absolve Me' made him a national figure, and 26 July named his movement.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Armistice ends the fighting in the Korean War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> signed at Panmunjom halted three years of war that had cost millions of lives. It created a demilitarised zone roughly along the 38th parallel, but no peace treaty has ever been signed.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-19">19 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Coup backed by the CIA and MI6 topples Iran's Mosaddegh</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Army units and paid crowds overthrew Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, who had nationalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s oil industry. The Shah's power was restored, and resentment of foreign meddling fed the 1979 revolution.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/land-to-the-tiller-act">Land-to-the-Tiller Act</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Land-to-the-Tiller programme of 1953 was the final stage of land reform in Taiwan under the Republic of China.</p></div></li></ol></section>
